Subject: Re: possible pkgsrc emacs framework improvements?
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.  The sweep line is still to be filled in.)

Hi Thomas,

Yes, I would like to work on this.  I have a prototype of all three
and have been building with it, so rather than say "sounds good" here
is what it does and what it turned out to cost.

  https://github.com/zakinko/pkgsrc-zakinko/tree/emacs-framework

I should say up front that I work in Japanese and am not strong in
English, so I write these with the help of a translation.  If
something reads oddly, or if I have misunderstood you, please tell me
plainly and I will rework it.

Version selection.  modules.mk now does what lang/python does:
EMACS_VERSION_REQD picks one version and refuses if the package cannot
use it, so a bulk pass leaves it alone instead of building it for
something else; otherwise EMACS_VERSION_DEFAULT (EMACS_TYPE keeps
working, it is just the default now); otherwise the first version the
package accepts.  _EMACS_VERSIONS_ALL is newest first, so "first
accepted" means the package's first choice, the way
PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED is written.

Choosing from EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED alone is not safe, which I did
not expect.  A package can name a version that has since gone -- after
emacs21 left 1.42, fifteen packages in my tree still ask for it -- and
selecting it leaves _EMACS_PKGDIR empty, so the include dies with
"Cannot open /version.mk" instead of failing cleanly.  The choice is
now made from the intersection of what the package accepts and what
modules.mk still lists, and a version that exists is kept so the
includes resolve while PKG_FAIL_REASON says what happened:

  devel/apel, EMACS_VERSION_REQD=emacs21
    "pkgsrc no longer has emacs21"
    "pkgsrc has: emacs31 emacs31nox ... xemacs214nox"

  devel/apel, EMACS_VERSION_REQD=emacs20
    "This package does not build with emacs20"
    "Accepted versions are: emacs29 emacs29nox ... emacs31nox"

Those two have different causes and different places to look, so they
do not share a sentence.  This is the case in your first point, seen
from the other side: www/emacs-w3m says it takes emacs21 while the
apel it depends on does not, and with this the build says so rather
than stopping the make.

Install location and name.  The lisp goes to
share/emacs/<version>/site-lisp, which Emacs already searches, and
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX is ${_EMACS_TYPE}-, so pcl-cvs becomes
emacs20-pcl-cvs and apel becomes emacs29-apel, emacs29nox-apel,
emacs30-apel and so on, as you described.

pbulk.  Three lines in mk/pbulk/pbulk-index.mk, the same shape as the
python and ruby entries.

What it costs.  I count 92 packages that read modules.mk, leaving out
wip and the emacs and xemacs packages themselves.

The install location is nearly free.  Of the 91 with a PLIST, 80
already write ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}; seven write the path by hand and
want changing (cqual, rsltc, jde, tamago-tsunagi, mailutils,
twittering-mode, emacs-dict-client).  Of the 32 that pass a lisp
directory into their own build, 31 already pass the variable.

The name is where the work is, and most of it is not in modules.mk.
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX only takes effect where the package spells it in
PKGNAME, and 22 of the 92 do not, so they still build as plain
dash-el, cqual, po-mode.  Adding it then moves PKGBASE, and two things
follow from that which I only found by building:

  Six packages take their distfile from GitHub without setting
  GITHUB_PROJECT, which defaults to ${PKGBASE}.  The fetch then asks
  for github.com/ziglang/emacs30nox-zig-mode and gets a 404, and
  WRKSRC points somewhere the archive does not unpack.  One line each
  fixes it: ecb, lua-mode, zig-mode, emacs-neotree, howm,
  markdown-mode.

  Six lines in the tree name a renamed package without the prefix and
  can then never be satisfied -- devel/flim adds an unprefixed
  apel>=10.8 to BUILDLINK_API_DEPENDS.apel while apel's own
  buildlink3.mk already says ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}apel>=10.  Three
  of the six are under wip.

I also had to fix two things in my own modules.mk that only building
showed.  EMACSLOADPATH for the build still named the old shared
site-lisp, so anything byte-compiling against another elisp package
stopped with "Cannot open load file"; and the EMACS_VERSION_REQD path
had the same empty-_EMACS_PKGDIR fatal I thought I had already fixed.

The part that cannot be found by reading at all is a package whose
PLIST uses the variable but whose install writes the path itself.
devel/pcl-cvs is one: nothing in its Makefile says lispdir, and the
bundled makefile has lispdir = $(datadir)/emacs/site-lisp.  So far
that is pcl-cvs, tamago, gnuserv and matlab-mode; the last two had
been writing the same directory two different ways for years, and
only agreed because the two spellings happened to produce the same
string.  I tried to find these by reading, with a filter over PLIST
and Makefile, and it gave both misses and false alarms and called 62
packages suspect, so I dropped it and built instead.

